    <body>
      <sec id="article-31142.s1" sec-type="pubmed-excerpt">
        <title>Introduction</title>
        <p>The vestibular system is fundamental for balance and spatial orientation, allowing&#x000a0;individuals to perceive and adapt to motion and positional changes in their environment. Starting with the inner ear,&#x000a0;the components of this organ system include the semicircular canals and otolith organs. These organs communicate through sensorineural pathways to multiple brain regions, coordinating both motor outputs and sensory integration (see <bold>Image</bold>.<bold>&#x000a0;</bold>Vestibulocochlear System Anatomy). Disorders involving the vestibular system have significant medical and surgical relevance, as lesions along either peripheral or central pathways manifest as vertigo, imbalance, and nystagmus.</p>
        <p>This&#x000a0;activity&#x000a0;integrates fundamental neuroanatomic principles with clinical correlations, addressing conditions such as vestibular schwannoma, oscillopsia, and Wallenberg syndrome, as well as peripheral disturbances&#x000a0;such as cerumen impaction and tympanic membrane perforation. A comprehensive understanding of vestibular anatomy and physiology enables clinicians to localize lesions accurately, differentiate among potential etiologies, and plan appropriate management. Mastery of these concepts supports diagnostic precision, guides surgical decision-making, and optimizes patient outcomes.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-31142.s2" sec-type="Structure and Function">
        <title>Structure and Function</title>
        <p>
<bold>Labyrinths</bold>
</p>
        <p>The labyrinth of the inner ear, located within the petrous portion of the temporal bone, comprises bony and membranous components. The bony labyrinth consists of the vestibule, cochlea, and&#x000a0;3 semicircular canals&#x02014; lateral, superior, and posterior&#x02014;all containing perilymph. Within the bony labyrinth lies the membranous labyrinth, which contains endolymph and includes the utricle, saccule, and their corresponding semicircular and cochlear ducts. The utricle and saccule, collectively termed the "otolith organs," detect static equilibrium and linear acceleration, while the semicircular ducts respond to rotational acceleration. Hair cells within the cristae ampullaris and maculae transduce mechanical motion into neural impulses that initiate transmission through the vestibular pathways.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31142.r1">[1]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Vestibular Pathways</bold>
</p>
        <p>Bipolar neurons of the Scarpa ganglion convey afferent signals from the otolith organs and semicircular canals through cranial nerve VIII (vestibulocochlear nerve) to the brainstem at the pontomedullary junction. These fibers synapse in the&#x000a0;4 vestibular nuclei&#x02014;superior, medial, lateral, and inferior&#x02014;or project directly to the cerebellum via the inferior cerebellar peduncle (juxtarestiform body). The vestibular nuclei give rise to multiple projection systems, including vestibulo-ocular tracts through the&#x000a0;medial longitudinal fasciculus (MLF) to cranial motor nuclei, vestibulospinal tracts to the spinal cord, cerebellar feedback circuits, and ascending reticulothalamic pathways toward cortical centers. Lesions involving the vestibular nuclei, particularly within the rostral medulla, may result in nystagmus, vertigo, postural instability, or facial paresthesia.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31142.r2">[2]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31142.r3">[3]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Spinal Cord</bold>
</p>
        <p>The vestibular system transmits descending motor signals through&#x000a0;2 principal tracts, the lateral (LVST) and medial (MVST)&#x000a0;vestibulospinal tracts. The LVST originates from the lateral vestibular nucleus and descends ipsilaterally through the anterior spinal cord to all levels, facilitating activation of extensor muscles in the limbs and trunk. This pathway maintains balance and posture by augmenting antigravity tone. The MVST arises from the medial vestibular nucleus and travels bilaterally via the MLF, projecting primarily to cervical spinal levels. This tract coordinates neck musculature, particularly the&#x000a0;sternocleidomastoid muscle, to stabilize the head during motion and contributes to the vestibulo-collic reflex.</p>
        <p>Damage to the LVST compromises postural control, producing truncal instability, falls, or lateropulsion. Affected individuals often demonstrate hypotonia of extensor muscles and diminished postural reflexes. Lesions involving the MVST impair head stabilization, disrupt vestibulo-collic reflexes, and interfere with gaze stabilization during rapid movements. These lesions may also reduce or abolish cervical vestibular-evoked myogenic potentials, indicating impaired activation of the&#x000a0;sternocleidomastoid.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31142.r4">[4]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31142.r5">[5]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31142.r6">[6]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31142.r7">[7]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Extraocular Motor Nuclei</bold>
</p>
        <p>The vestibular nuclei transmit essential signals to the ocular motor centers, namely,&#x000a0;cranial nerves III (oculomotor), IV (trochlear), and VI (abducens), through ascending fibers coursing within the MLF. Activation of the superior and medial vestibular nuclei during head rotation generates impulses that cross to the contralateral abducens nucleus, stimulating the lateral rectus muscle, while interneuronal projections via the MLF activate the ipsilateral&#x000a0;oculomotor nucleus to contract the medial rectus. This bilateral coordination produces conjugate eye movements opposite to head motion, preserving visual stability through the vestibulo-ocular reflex (VOR).<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31142.r8">[8]</xref></p>
        <p>The trochlear&#x000a0;nerve, arising from its nucleus in the dorsal midbrain, supplies the contralateral superior oblique muscle.&#x000a0;This nerve contributes to vertical gaze and torsional eye movements, enabling fine ocular adjustments during head tilt or rotation across multiple planes. Although its role in horizontal gaze is indirect,&#x000a0;the trochlear nerve is critical for maintaining visual field stability during complex dynamic motion.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31142.r9">[9]</xref></p>
        <p>Lesions involving the MLF or its projections from the vestibular nuclei give rise to well-defined clinical syndromes. The characteristic manifestation of internuclear ophthalmoplegia is impaired adduction of the ipsilateral eye, accompanied by abducting nystagmus of the contralateral eye during horizontal gaze. Disruption of the horizontal&#x000a0;VOR pathway produces horizontal diplopia and impaired gaze holding.</p>
        <p>Bilateral MLF involvement typically results in pronounced horizontal gaze limitation and gait instability.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31142.r10">[10]</xref> Lesions affecting the abducens nucleus, rather than the nerve itself, produce a horizontal gaze palsy involving both eyes toward the side of the lesion. This&#x000a0;deficit occurs due to the interruption of interneuronal fibers projecting to the contralateral oculomotor nucleus.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31142.r11">[11]</xref> Lesions of the trochlear nucleus manifest as vertical diplopia and torsional ocular misalignment, most evident during downgaze or when descending stairs, secondary to dysfunction of the superior oblique muscle.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31142.r12">[12]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Thalamus</bold>
</p>
        <p>The thalamus functions as a central relay for vestibular processing, receiving bilateral input from the superior vestibular nuclei and contralateral projections from the medial vestibular nucleus via the MLF and central tegmental tracts. Within the thalamus, vestibular signals are primarily processed in the ventral posterior complex, which comprises the ventral posterolateral, ventral posteromedial, and ventral posteroinferior nuclei. These nuclei integrate vestibular, proprioceptive, and visual inputs and transmit efferent fibers through the posterior limb of the internal capsule to the primary somatosensory cortex. Subsequent projections to the primary motor and posterior parietal cortices contribute to conscious spatial orientation, postural regulation, and perception of head and body position in space.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31142.r13">[13]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31142.r14">[14]</xref></p>
        <p>Lesions involving vestibular-responsive thalamic regions impair sensory integration and distort spatial perception. Affected individuals may exhibit a tilted subjective visual vertical, postural imbalance, gait instability, or lateropulsion, the tendency to fall or lean to one side despite preserved motor strength. These manifestations reflect disrupted cortical processing of vestibular input rather than peripheral vestibular dysfunction, underscoring the thalamus as a key structure in higher-order spatial orientation and postural control.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31142.r15">[15]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Cerebellum</bold>
</p>
        <p>The vestibulocerebellum, comprising the flocculonodular lobe and adjacent vermis, maintains an exclusive functional connection with the vestibular system.&#x000a0;This&#x000a0;region receives primary afferent input directly from the Scarpa ganglion and secondary projections from the vestibular nuclei through the juxtarestiform body of the inferior cerebellar peduncle.</p>
        <p>Efferent fibers from the vestibulocerebellum project back to the vestibular nuclei, influencing both spinal and ocular motor activity. This bidirectional circuitry regulates balance, calibrates the VOR, adjusts posture, and facilitates motor learning.</p>
        <p>The flocculus contributes to smooth pursuit and adaptive modulation of the VOR, whereas the nodular regions regulate equilibrium and spatial orientation. Lesions involving these regions, particularly within the flocculonodular lobe, produce gaze-holding deficits, disrupted smooth tracking, nystagmus, and truncal ataxia, often presenting as a broad-based or &#x0201c;drunken&#x0201d; gait.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31142.r16">[16]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31142.r17">[17]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Cerebral Cortex</bold>
</p>
        <p>Vestibular input relayed through the thalamus is transmitted to higher-order cortical centers, most prominently converging in the parieto-insular vestibular cortex. Located in the posterior insula and parietal operculum, the parieto-insular vestibular cortex serves as a multisensory integration zone where vestibular, visual, and somatosensory information merge to construct a unified perception of spatial orientation and motion awareness.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31142.r18">[18]</xref>&#x000a0;The posterior parietal cortex further transforms these integrated signals into spatial reference frameworks necessary for movement coordination and navigation.</p>
        <p>Additional cortical areas, including the cingulate sulcus visual area, precuneus motion area, and vestibular-responsive pericallosal sulcus of the medial cortex, participate in self-motion perception and visual-vestibular integration.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31142.r19">[19]</xref> These interconnected cortical networks collectively sustain balance, gaze control, motion perception, and body orientation. Lesions involving cortical vestibular pathways lead to perceptual disturbances of verticality, impaired motion awareness, and spatial disorientation, reflecting disruption of higher-order cortical integration of vestibular information.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31142.r20">[20]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-31142.s3" sec-type="Embryology">
        <title>Embryology</title>
        <p>The vestibular system, similar to the auditory apparatus, originates from the otic placode&#x02014;a thickening of ectodermal tissue that appears around the 3rd week of embryonic development. The otic placode invaginates to form the otic cup, which subsequently pinches off to create the otic vesicle, or otocyst.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31142.r21">[21]</xref> The otic vesicle serves as the embryonic precursor of the inner ear and gives rise to both vestibular and auditory components of the membranous labyrinth.</p>
        <p>During further development, neuroblasts within the otic vesicle differentiate to form the statoacoustic ganglion, which provides sensory innervation to the inner ear. In the early stages, the neurogenic region of the otic vesicle primarily produces neurons destined for the vestibular system, while auditory neurons emerge later. This sequence is governed by multiple signaling pathways and transcription factors, including fibroblast growth factor (FGF), wingless-related integration site (WNT), bone morphogenetic protein (BMP), Neurogenin1, NeuroD, and GATA-binding protein 3 (GATA3), that orchestrate spatial and temporal patterning within the vesicle.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31142.r22">[22]</xref></p>
        <p>Morphogenesis of the otic vesicle leads to the formation of the utricle, saccule, semicircular canals, and cochlear duct (see <bold>Image</bold>.&#x000a0;Vestibular System Development in Embryonic Stages).<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31142.r23">[23]</xref> The dorsolateral region of the vesicle develops into the vestibular ganglion, whereas the ventromedial domain gives rise to the cochlear (spiral) ganglion. This differentiation establishes the structural and functional division between the vestibular and auditory systems.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31142.r24">[24]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-31142.s4" sec-type="Blood Supply and Lymphatics">
        <title>Blood Supply and Lymphatics</title>
        <p>The vestibular system receives its arterial supply primarily from the labyrinthine artery, which most often originates from the anterior inferior cerebellar artery (AICA) and, less frequently, from the basilar artery. Upon entering the inner ear through the internal auditory meatus with the facial nerve (cranial nerve VII) and vestibulocochlear nerve, the labyrinthine artery divides into the anterior vestibular, cochlear, and vestibulocochlear (posterior vestibular) arteries.</p>
        <p>The anterior vestibular artery supplies the utricle, the ampullae of the anterior and lateral semicircular canals, and a portion of the vestibular nerve. The cochlear artery provides vascular supply to the cochlea, whereas the vestibulocochlear artery serves the saccule and posterior semicircular canal (see <bold>Image</bold>.&#x000a0;Blood Supply and Innervation of Vestibular Sensory Organs).<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31142.r25">[25]</xref> Venous drainage occurs through the labyrinthine vein, which empties into either the inferior petrosal or sigmoid sinus.</p>
        <p>The posterior inferior cerebellar artery (PICA), a branch of the vertebral artery, supplies adjacent structures, including the vestibular nuclei, inferior cerebellar peduncle, and nodulus.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31142.r26">[26]</xref>&#x000a0;An AICA infarction may&#x000a0;produce vertigo, hearing loss, and facial weakness, whereas a PICA occlusion can produce Wallenberg syndrome.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31142.r27">[27]</xref></p>
        <p>The vestibular system lacks true lymphatic vessels but depends on&#x000a0;2 distinct fluid systems for drainage and homeostasis. Endolymph drains from the membranous labyrinth into the endolymphatic sac, which contributes to fluid absorption, pressure regulation, and immune defense. Perilymph, situated between the bony and membranous labyrinths, communicates with the subarachnoid space through the cochlear aqueduct, permitting pressure equilibration with cerebrospinal fluid. These mechanisms preserve the inner ear&#x02019;s fluid balance and functional integrity in lieu of conventional lymphatic drainage.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31142.r28">[28]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-31142.s5" sec-type="Nerves">
        <title>Nerves</title>
        <p>
<bold>Vestibulocochlear Nerve</bold>
</p>
        <p>The vestibulocochlear nerve conveys sensory input related to balance and hearing. Vestibular signals arise from specialized inner ear structures, namely the semicircular canals, utricle, and saccule. These signals travel through the vestibular division of the vestibulocochlear nerve via the internal acoustic meatus, which it shares with the facial nerve (see <bold>Image</bold>.&#x000a0;Anatomy of the Vestibulocochlear Nerve and Associated Structures). The vestibular nerve fibers enter the brainstem at the pontomedullary junction and project to the vestibular nuclei, near the inferior cerebellar peduncle, or to the flocculonodular lobe of the cerebellum. Schwannomas originating from the vestibular portion of the vestibulocochlear nerve may cause dizziness, imbalance, and hearing loss due to compressive effects on the nerve (see <bold>Clinical Significance</bold> section).<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31142.r29">[29]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31142.r30">[30]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Abducens Nerve</bold>
</p>
        <p>The abducens nerve innervates the lateral rectus muscle, which abducts the eye. During head rotation, such as to the left, vestibular impulses are transmitted from the left semicircular canal to the left vestibular nuclei. These nuclei send excitatory projections to the right abducens nucleus, activating the right lateral rectus muscle. Concurrently, interneurons ascend in the MLF to stimulate the left oculomotor nucleus, coordinating conjugate eye movement opposite to the head turn. Simultaneous inhibitory signals to opposing muscles maintain smooth and stable horizontal gaze.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31142.r31">[31]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Oculomotor Nerve</bold>
</p>
        <p>The oculomotor nerve supplies multiple extraocular muscles, including the medial rectus, which adducts the eye. During a head rotation to the left, input from the vestibular apparatus activates the left medial vestibular nucleus, which transmits excitatory signals through the MLF to the left oculomotor nucleus. Activation of the left medial rectus muscle directs the left eye rightward, ensuring conjugate gaze opposite to the direction of head movement.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31142.r32">[32]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Trochlear Nerve</bold>
</p>
        <p>The trochlear nerve innervates the superior oblique muscle of the contralateral eye, enabling intorsion, depression, and lateral rotation. This function supports precise vertical and torsional adjustments required for coordinated tracking and gaze stabilization during head motion.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31142.r33">[33]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-31142.s6" sec-type="Physiologic Variants">
        <title>Physiologic Variants</title>
        <p>The vestibular system exhibits a broad spectrum of physiologic, anatomic, and vascular variants that hold clinical and imaging significance. Anatomic variants include an enlarged vestibular aqueduct, which may present as a benign finding or occur in association with hearing loss.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31142.r34">[34]</xref> Hypoplasia or aplasia of the semicircular canals, particularly the posterior canal, may occur as isolated anomalies or components of congenital syndromes.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31142.r35">[35]</xref></p>
        <p>Semicircular canal dehiscence, most frequently involving the superior canal, represents another recognized variant that may remain asymptomatic or cause vestibular disturbances when the bony roof is thinned or absent.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31142.r36">[36]</xref> Variations in the size, trajectory, or branching pattern of the vestibulocochlear nerve, including duplications or absence of distinct divisions, have been described and may affect vestibular assessment and cochlear implantation outcomes.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31142.r37">[37]</xref></p>
        <p>Vascular variants are also well documented, with the labyrinthine artery, typically a branch of the AICA, arising directly from the basilar artery in approximately 10% to 15% of individuals.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31142.r38">[38]</xref> Variability in the branching of the anterior vestibular and common cochlear arteries may alter regional perfusion and influence vulnerability to ischemia within inner ear structures. Awareness of these variants is essential to prevent diagnostic errors and guide management in patients with vertigo, sensorineural hearing loss, or planned&#x000a0;otologic or neurosurgical procedures.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31142.r39">[39]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-31142.s7" sec-type="Surgical Considerations">
        <title>Surgical Considerations</title>
        <p>Surgical procedures involving the vestibular system, including vestibular schwannoma resection and cochlear implantation, demand meticulous planning due to the complex neurovascular anatomy of the internal auditory canal and temporal bone. A detailed understanding of the labyrinthine artery&#x02019;s anatomic variations, particularly its origin and branching into anterior vestibular and cochlear vessels, is critical to minimize the risk of intraoperative complications such as hearing loss and postoperative vertigo.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31142.r40">[40]</xref></p>
        <p>Recent advances in augmented reality and 3-dimensional visualization technologies provide enhanced preoperative planning tools, especially for vestibular schwannoma surgery. These systems allow precise overlay of patient-specific anatomy onto the surgical field, improving spatial orientation and intraoperative accuracy.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31142.r41">[41]</xref></p>
        <p>During cochlear implantation, careful selection of electrode insertion technique, whether through a cochleostomy or the round window, significantly influences the integrity of vestibular structures, such as the saccule, and affects postoperative balance outcomes.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31142.r42">[42]</xref> Although cochlear implantation is generally well tolerated, transient disequilibrium can occur, and vestibular function preservation is achieved more frequently with minimally invasive approaches.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31142.r43">[43]</xref>&#x000a0;Comprehensive knowledge of surgical anatomy, combined with advanced imaging and navigation technologies, enables a systems-based operative strategy that optimizes functional preservation and reduces vestibular morbidity.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-31142.s8" sec-type="Clinical Significance">
        <title>Clinical Significance</title>
        <p>
<bold>Vestibular Schwannoma</bold>
</p>
        <p>Vestibular schwannomas (acoustic neuromas) are benign neoplasms arising from Schwann cells of the vestibular branch of the vestibulocochlear nerve. These tumors most commonly develop in the cerebellopontine angle, the region between the cerebellum and the pons (see <bold>Image</bold>.&#x000a0;Contrast-Enhanced Magnetic Resonance Image of Vestibular Schwannoma).</p>
        <p>Progressive unilateral sensorineural hearing loss and tinnitus represent the characteristic initial manifestations, resulting from compression of the cochlear portion of the vestibulocochlear nerve. Vestibular disturbances, such as vertigo&#x000a0;and imbalance, may occur but are less frequent. Tumor enlargement can exert mass effect on the cerebellar flocculus, producing nystagmus. In advanced cases, involvement of adjacent cranial nerves may result in facial paralysis from facial nerve compression or facial sensory deficits from trigeminal nerve involvement.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31142.r44">[44]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31142.r45">[45]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Oscillopsia</bold>
</p>
        <p>Oscillopsia arises from VOR dysfunction, which normally stabilizes retinal images during head movement. VOR deficiency, particularly when bilateral, causes the visual scene to appear to move with the head, producing a sensation of visual instability. This phenomenon impairs the ability to maintain visual focus during motion and is frequently associated with vertigo, dizziness, and imbalance.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Wallenberg Syndrome</bold>
</p>
        <p>Wallenberg syndrome, or lateral medullary infarction, results from PICA occlusion, leading to ischemic injury involving the vestibular nuclei, inferior cerebellar peduncle, spinal trigeminal nucleus, and posterior cerebellum (see <bold>Image</bold>.&#x000a0;Right Wallenberg Syndrome on Magnetic Resonance Imaging). The condition produces vertigo, nausea, vomiting, and ipsilateral limb or truncal ataxia. Patients typically fall toward the side of the lesion and demonstrate ipsilateral facial pain and temperature sensory loss due to trigeminal nucleus involvement. Oculomotor abnormalities, including nystagmus, may also occur depending on the extent and location of the lesion.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31142.r46">[46]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-31142.s9" sec-type="Other Issues">
        <title>Other Issues</title>
        <p>Peripheral conditions that influence vestibular function include cerumen impaction and tympanic membrane perforation. These disorders may produce dizziness or imbalance through mechanical obstruction, pressure alteration, or secondary irritation of adjacent structures. Although not primary vestibular pathologies, these&#x000a0;clinical entities&#x000a0;are essential considerations in the assessment of nonspecific vertigo. Awareness of the effects of these conditions underscores the functional interdependence of the auditory and vestibular systems and supports a systems-based approach to evaluating and managing dizziness.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Cerumen Impaction</bold>
</p>
        <p>Impacted cerumen may affect vestibular function through indirect mechanisms such as occlusion of the external auditory canal, irritation of the tympanic membrane, or caloric stimulation caused by improper irrigation.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31142.r47">[47]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31142.r48">[48]</xref>&#x000a0;Vertigo is an uncommon but documented manifestation that typically resolves following cerumen removal. Clinical observations and patient-reported experiences reinforce this relationship, highlighting the importance of otoscopic examination in individuals presenting with dizziness. Safe removal techniques, particularly irrigation with body-temperature solutions, are critical to prevent iatrogenic vestibular stimulation.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31142.r49">[49]</xref> Recognition of cerumen impaction as a potential contributor to imbalance enables timely intervention and helps avoid unnecessary diagnostic procedures or specialist referrals. See <bold>Image.</bold>&#x000a0;Impacted Cerumen.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Tympanic Membrane Perforation</bold>
</p>
        <p>A tympanic membrane perforation, often termed a "ruptured eardrum," commonly arises from otitis media, trauma, barotrauma, or inappropriate ear cleaning.&#x000a0;The condition typically presents with acute conductive hearing loss, tinnitus, otalgia, and, occasionally, vertigo or imbalance secondary to inner ear involvement or perilymph fistula formation.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31142.r50">[50]</xref> Most perforations close spontaneously within several weeks to months.&#x000a0;However, persistent vertigo, large defects, or delayed healing may necessitate patching or tympanoplasty.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31142.r51">[51]</xref></p>
        <p>Comprehensive evaluation includes otoscopic examination, audiometric testing, and a detailed clinical history. Urgent otolaryngology referral is warranted when significant hearing loss, vertigo, facial nerve dysfunction, or suspected perilymph leak is present. Maintaining a dry ear and avoiding both water exposure and ototoxic ear drops are essential during recovery to promote healing and prevent complications.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-31142.s10">
        <title>Review Questions</title>
        <list list-type="bullet">
