    <back>
      <floats-group id="article-31142.s11">
        <fig id="article-31142.image.f1" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <p>Vestibular System Development in Embryonic Stages.&#x000a0;This image illustrates the progressive development of the vestibular labyrinth from the neural tube stage through multiple embryonic stages. The utricle, saccule, cochlea, and semicircular canals are visible with color differentiation of key components. Contributed by William Gossman Collection</p>
          </caption>
          <graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="Screen__Shot__2016-08-10__at__9.53.56__PM" position="float" orientation="portrait"/>
        </fig>
      </floats-group>
      <floats-group id="article-31142.s12">
        <fig id="article-31142.image.f2" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <p>Vestibulocochlear System Anatomy. This&#x000a0;labeled schematic shows key components of the vestibulocochlear system, including semicircular canals, cochlea, auditory and vestibular nerves, and outer ear structures. The inset highlights&#x000a0;the utricle detailing nerve fiber connections. Contributed by Ella Workman</p>
          </caption>
          <graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="Vestibulocochlear__System" position="float" orientation="portrait"/>
        </fig>
      </floats-group>
      <floats-group id="article-31142.s13">
        <fig id="article-31142.image.f3" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <p>Blood Supply and Innervation of Vestibular Sensory Organs.&#x000a0;This schematic highlights vestibular nerve branches and key vascular arteries supplying the utricle, saccule, and semicircular canals, emphasizing the relationship between circulatory and neural pathways. Tanner Jefferies, Medical Student</p>
          </caption>
          <graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="Blood__supply__to__the__Vestibular__system" position="float" orientation="portrait"/>
        </fig>
      </floats-group>
      <floats-group id="article-31142.s14">
        <fig id="article-31142.image.f4" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <p>Contrast-Enhanced Magnetic Resonance Image of Vestibular Schwannoma.&#x000a0;Axial T1-weighted contrast-enhanced&#x000a0;magnetic resonance image demonstrates a well-circumscribed enhancing mass (arrowhead) within the right internal auditory canal extending toward the cerebellopontine angle. The lesion arises from the vestibular division of the vestibulocochlear nerve and exhibits homogeneous enhancement with mild mass effect on adjacent cerebellar structures. Contributed by Dawood Tafti, MD.</p>
          </caption>
          <graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="porus__acousticus__vestibular__schwannoma" position="float" orientation="portrait"/>
        </fig>
      </floats-group>
      <floats-group id="article-31142.s15">
        <fig id="article-31142.image.f5" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <p>Right Wallenberg Syndrome on Magnetic Resonance Imaging.&#x000a0;Axial magnetic resonance images demonstrate a hyperintense lesion in the right dorsolateral medulla on T2-weighted (left) and fluid-attenuated inversion recovery (FLAIR, middle) sequences, with corresponding restricted diffusion on diffusion-weighted imaging (right). Findings are consistent with an acute right lateral medullary infarction, or Wallenberg syndrome, typically secondary to occlusion of the posterior inferior cerebellar artery or a branch of the vertebral artery. Contributed by Sunil Munakomi, MD</p>
          </caption>
          <graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="right__wallenberg" position="float" orientation="portrait"/>
        </fig>
      </floats-group>
      <floats-group id="article-31142.s16">
        <fig id="article-31142.image.f6" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <p>Anatomy of the Vestibulocochlear Nerve and Associated Structures. This schematic illustration shows the course of the vestibulocochlear nerve (cranial nerve&#x000a0;VIII) from the inner ear to the brainstem. The vestibular and cochlear divisions convey balance and auditory information, respectively. The image also shows the semicircular canals, vestibule, cochlea, tympanic membrane, ossicles, and Eustachian tube in relation to the pons and medulla oblongata. Tanner Jefferies, Medical Student</p>
          </caption>
          <graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="xVestibularcochlear__nerve__anatomy" position="float" orientation="portrait"/>
        </fig>
      </floats-group>
      <floats-group id="article-31142.s17">
        <fig id="article-31142.image.f7" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <p>Impacted Cerumen. Hardened earwax blocks the ear canal and may cause hearing loss, ear fullness, pain, vertigo, or tinnitus until safely removed by a professional. Schwartz SR, Magit AE, Rosenfeld RM, et al. Clinical practice guideline (update): earwax (cerumen impaction). <italic toggle="yes">Otolaryngol Head Neck Surg.</italic> 2017;156(suppl 1):S1-S29. doi: 10.1177/0194599816671491.</p>
          </caption>
          <graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="Otoscopic-view-of-impacted-cerumen-mixed-with-hair-completely-obstructing-the-ear" position="float" orientation="portrait"/>
        </fig>
      </floats-group>
